{"records":[{"id":"332n4pdxh9","title":"Arthritis Flares Mediated by Tissue Resident Memory T Cells in the Joint","description":"Rheumatoid arthritis is a systemic autoimmune disease, but disease flares typically affect only a subset of joints, distributed in a pattern that is distinctive for each patient. Pursuing this intriguing pattern, we show that arthritis recurrence is mediated by long-lived synovial resident memory T cells (TRM). In three murine models, CD8+ cells bearing TRM markers remain in previously inflamed joints during remission. These cells are bona fide TRM, exhibiting failure to migrate from joint to joint, preferential uptake of fatty acids, and long-term residency. Disease flares result from TRM activation by antigen, leading to CCL5-mediated recruitment of circulating effector cells. Correspondingly, TRM depletion ameliorates recurrence in a site-specific manner. Human rheumatoid arthritis joint tissues contain a comparable CD8+-predominant TRM population, most evident in late-stage leukocyte-poor synovium, exhibiting limited T cell receptor diversity and a pro-inflammatory transcriptomic signature. Together, these findings establish synovial TRM cells as a targetable mediator of disease chronicity in autoimmune arthritis. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-10-20T08:04:35.403Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/332n4pdxh9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/332n4pdxh9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Margaret H","last_name":"Chang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Arthritis","Memory"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f52y9zkpzm","title":"RNF31 inhibition sensitizes tumors to bystander killing by innate and adaptive immune cells","description":"Tumor escape mechanisms for immunotherapy include deficiencies in antigen presentation, diminishing adaptive CD8+ T cell antitumor activity. Although innate NK cells are triggered by loss of MHC class I, their response is often inadequate. To increase tumor susceptibility to both innate and adaptive immune elimination, we performed parallel genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens under NK and CD8+ T cell pressure. We identified all components, RNF31, RBCK1, and SHARPIN, of the linear ubiquitination chain assembly complex (LUBAC). Genetic and pharmacologic ablation of RNF31, an E3 ubiquitin ligase, strongly sensitized melanoma, breast and colorectal cancer cells to both NK and CD8+ T cell killing. This occurred in a TNF-dependent manner, causing loss of A20 and non-canonical IKK complexes from TNF Receptor Complex I. Corroborating this preclinically, a small molecule RNF31 inhibitor sensitized human colon carcinoma organoids to TNF and greatly enhanced immune bystander killing of antigen-loss and antigen presentation machinery-deficient tumor cells. These results merit exploration of RNF31 inhibition as a clinical pharmacological opportunity for immunotherapy-refractory cancers.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-05-30T07:01:54.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f52y9zkpzm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f52y9zkpzm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhengkui","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer Immunology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"v7jysndj3m","title":"A GAPDH serotonylation system couples CD8+ T-cell glycolytic metabolism to antitumor immunity","description":"The neuroimmune signaling axis involving serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine, 5-HT) has been well acknowledged. Apart from the canonical 5-HT-receptor signaling transduction pattern, 5-HT-involved post-translational serotonylation has recently been noted. However, whether and how serotonylation affects immune cell function remains poorly understood. Using copper click chemistry and an unbiased proteomic approach, glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase (GAPDH) was identified as the substrate for serotonylation in activated CD8+ T cells. Tissue transglutaminase 2 (TGM2) catalyzed the serotonylation reaction of GAPDH by covalently incorporating intracellular 5-HT into GAPDH Gln262 (Q262ser), resulting in enhanced cytoplasmic translocation and glycolytic activity. Glycolytic metabolism induced by GAPDH serotonylation supports the expression of the cytokine interferon γ (IFN-γ) and contributes to the antitumor ability of CD8+ T cells. Activated CD8+ T cells accumulate intracellular 5-HT for serotonylation through both cell-autonomous synthesis by tryptophan hydroxylase 1 (TPH1) and uptake from the extracellular compartment via serotonin transporter (SERT). Genetic ablation of TPH1 or SERT inhibits proliferation and IFN-γ production in CD8+ T cells, which subsequently impairs their antitumor immune activity. Monoamine oxidase A (MAOA) degrades intracellular 5-HT and acts as an intrinsic negative regulator of CD8+ T cells. Adoptive transfer of 5-HT-producing TPH1-overexpressing chimeric antigen receptor T (CAR-T) cells induced a robust antitumor response in mice. These findings reveal a GAPDH serotonylation system that promotes glycolytic metabolism and antitumor immune activity of CD8+ T cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-04T08:54:38.71Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/v7jysndj3m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/v7jysndj3m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xu","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhi-Gang","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":"0000-0001-8965-223X"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Tumor","Serotonin","Immunity","Cytotoxic T-Cell"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rg353wcrwr","title":"CD147 regulates CD8+ T cell cytotoxic activity by facilitating phosphorylation and subsequent recruitment of proximal TCR signaling kinases(ISCIENCE-D-25-09187)","description":"Doxycycline Inducible Chimeric Antigen Receptor T Cells Targeting CD147 for Hepatocellular Carcinoma Therapy","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-02T12:05:24.23Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rg353wcrwr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rg353wcrwr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"taiyu","last_name":"shu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer Immunology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6vr8v59ykm","title":"Chikungunya virus infection disrupts MHC-I antigen presentation via nonstructural protein 2 ","description":"Arthritogenic alphaviruses, including chikungunya virus (CHIKV), are re-emerging global public health threats with no approved vaccines or antiviral therapies. Infection with these viruses causes debilitating musculoskeletal disease for months to years that is associated with the persistence of viral RNA and antigen. Prior studies using a mouse model found that CD8+ T cells, which recognize viral peptides in the context of major histocompatibility class I (MHC-I) displayed on the surface of infected cells, have a limited role in the control and clearance of CHIKV infection in joint-associated tissues, suggesting that CHIKV-infected cells evade these critical effectors of the antiviral immune response. Here, we show that MHC-I antigen presentation is inefficient in CHIKV-infected joint tissue fibroblasts, and that a protein encoded by CHIKV and produced in infected cells, nonstructural protein 2 (nsP2), disrupts the surface display of MHC-I molecules and antigen recognition of infected cells by CD8+ T cells. Our findings support a role for CHIKV nsP2 in the evasion of the CD8+ T cell response and establishment of persistent infection.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-12T19:51:36.848Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6vr8v59ykm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6vr8v59ykm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Tem","last_name":"Morrison","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Flow Cytometry","Antigen Presentation","Major Histocompatibility Complex","Cytotoxic T-Cell","Chikungunya Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6m6bt5427c","title":"Supplementary data for 'Compositional heterogeneity in 91500, GJ-1/89 and TEMORA-2 zircon reference materials'","description":"Solution Q-ICP-MS data for GJ-1/89 zircon and long-term laser ablation Q-ICP-MS data for 91500, GJ-1/89 and TEMORA-2 zircon reference materials.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-30T20:52:45.2Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6m6bt5427c","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6m6bt5427c"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"John","last_name":"Caulfield","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Charlotte M","last_name":"ALLEN","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Teresa","last_name":"Ubide","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ai","last_name":"Nguyen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Henrietta","last_name":"Cathey","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/05mmh0f86","name":"Australian Research Council","grant_id":"LP210200793"},{"id":"https://ror.org/05mmh0f86","name":"Australian Research Council","grant_id":"ARC FT230100230; RM 2023000028"}],"categories":["Laser Ablation Inductively Coupled Plasma Mass Spectrometry","Reference Material","Trace Element","Zircon"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tgsc7w7sdr","title":"Long-term Selective Stimulation of Transplanted Neural Stem/Progenitor Cells for Spinal Cord Injury Improves Locomotor Function. Kawai et al.","description":"Source data for \"Long-term Selective Stimulation of Transplanted Neural Stem/Progenitor Cells for Spinal Cord Injury Improves Locomotor Function\". Further information and requests for resources and reagents should be directed to and will be fulfilled by the lead contact.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-15T13:42:47.284Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tgsc7w7sdr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tgsc7w7sdr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Momotaro","last_name":"Kawai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Induced Pluripotent Stem Cell","Spinal Cord Injury","Stem Cell Transplant Related Therapy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xbvcsdp86v","title":"Intratumoral IL-12 delivery empowers CAR-T cell immunotherapy in a pre-clinical model of glioblastoma","description":"Giulia Agliardi*, Anna Rita Liuzzi*, Alastair Hotblack, Donatella De Feo, Nicolás Núñez, Cassandra L. Stowe, Ekaterina Friebel, Francesco Nannini, Lukas Rindlisbacher, Thomas A. Roberts, Rajiv Ramasawmy,  Iwan P. Williams, Bernard M. Siow, Mark F. Lythgoe, Tammy L. Kalber, Sergio A. Quezada, Martin A. Pule, Sonia Tugues, Karin Straathof§ and Burkhard Becher§.\n\n*These authors contributed equally\n§ These authors contributed equally\n\nCorresponding authors:\nBurkhard Becher. E-Mail: becher@immunology.uzh.ch\nKarin Straathof. E-Mail: k.straathof@ucl.ac.uk\n\nGlioblastoma multiforme (GBM) is the most common and aggressive form of primary brain\ncancer, for which effective therapies are urgently needed. Chimeric antigen receptor (CAR)-\nbased immunotherapy represents a promising therapeutic approach, but it is often impeded\nby highly immunosuppressive tumor microenvironments (TME). Here, in an immunocompetent,\northotopic GBM mouse model, we show that CAR-T cells targeting tumorspecific\nepidermal growth factor variant III (EGFRvIII) alone fail to control fully established\ntumors but, when combined with a single, locally delivered dose of IL-12, achieve durable antitumor\nresponses. IL-12 not only boosts cytotoxicity of CAR-T cells, but also reshapes the\nTME, driving increased infiltration of proinflammatory CD4+ T cells, decreased numbers of\nregulatory T cells (Treg), and activation of the myeloid compartment. Importantly, the\nimmunotherapy-enabling benefits of IL-12 are achieved with minimal systemic effects. Our\nfindings thus show that local delivery of IL-12 may be an effective adjuvant for CAR-T cell\ntherapy for GBM.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-01-04T10:12:50.015Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xbvcsdp86v","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xbvcsdp86v"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Anna Rita","last_name":"Liuzzi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Giulia","last_name":"Agliardi","orcid":null},{"first_name":" Karin","last_name":"Straathof","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Burkhard","last_name":"Becher","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Microglia","Brain Cancer","Lymphocyte","Myeloid Cell","Flow Cytometry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rc34b77gs6","title":"NCT02434354 Neoadjuvant anti-PD-1 Pembrolizumab Clinical Trial","description":"Flow cytometry data for NCT02434354 Neoadjuvant anti-PD-1 Pembrolizumab Clinical Trial.\nAntigen-specific profiling identifies T-bet+ melanoma-specific CD8+ T cells associated with pathologic response to neoadjuvant anti-PD-1 therapy\nCancer Cell 2025","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-12-08T12:23:01.459Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rc34b77gs6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rc34b77gs6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Guanning","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexander","last_name":"Huang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Flow Cytometry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"53j3vrhddj","title":"Opposing regulation of the STING pathway in hepatic stellate cells by NBR1 and p62 determines the progression of hepatocellular carcinoma","description":"Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) emerges from chronic inflammation to which activation of hepatic stellate cells (HSCs) contributes by shaping a pro-tumorigenic microenvironment. Key to this process is p62, whose inactivation leads to enhanced hepatocarcinogenesis. Here, we show that while p62 positively regulates STING ubiquitination by TRIM32 by displacing NBR1, which results in the activation of the interferon (IFN) cascade, NBR1 prevents TRIM32 interaction with and the activation of STING, leading to impaired IFN synthesis. NBR1 also antagonizes STING function by promoting its trafficking from the Golgi to the endosome-lysosomal degradative cascade independent of autophagy. Importantly, NBR1 deletion completely reverts the tumor-promoting function of p62-deficient HSCs. The upregulation of the STING-IFN pathway by NBR1 deficiency enhances the anti-tumor response mediated by CD8+ T cells. These results identify NBR1 as a synthetic vulnerability of p62-deficiency in HSCs. NBR1 loss, by promoting the STING/IFN pathway, boosts anti-tumor CD8+ T cell responses to restrain HCC.\nNbr1 knock-out and wild-type hepatic stellate cells were profiled in three biological replicates under normal conditions.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-09-20T18:33:10.351Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/53j3vrhddj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/53j3vrhddj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Moscat","last_name":"Diaz-Meco","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Interferon","Hepatocellular Carcinoma","Hepatic Stellate Cell"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":134654,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":105,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":134654,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":133922,"restricted":68,"embargoed":664},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2247},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1346},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":670},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":468},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":425},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":414},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":405},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":398},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":329},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":267}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":132026},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1900},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":185},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":131},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":75},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":56},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":37},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":27}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":874},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":795},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":512},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":356}]}}